\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Airborne usually kept their feet where evolution never intended them: planted in nothing but expensive air. The ground was for the underclass, and the less said about them, the better. But there was ground, and there was ground\'97anyone could buy height; there was always another taller tower on the drawing board, and climbing into the sky was the one thing the meritocracy could always make more of. Which was exactly why, by market arithmetic, it counted for less every year. What nobody could make was more harbour. Or more beach. There was only so much water\'92s edge, and every inch of it had been bought, married into, and quietly litigated and killed over long before the first Airborne ate breakfast at altitude. It was scarce, 
\f2\i and 
\f1\i0 it had views. Not just view projection windows that simulated outlooks over anything the world had to offer, but real views from real places that were not bought and sold so much as quietly bid for (usually in some of the most prestigious art auction houses in the world), provided there was not a Wanksy tag in sight. Like old money, new money went up, but old money kept the ground as well, and anyone who counted could tell the two apart at a glance.\
Back before Jamison Hardy joined the Mergers and Acquisitions Youth, before the Layer had become reality, old money funded both Layer denialism and a campaign for the government to protect \'93culturally significant landmarks\'94\'97those being their Eastern Suburbs mansions, apartments, and bachelor pads, as well as all beaches south to Coogee. By necessity (and contrived exclusivity) the border demarcations between \'93culturally significant\'94 and \'93snob-adjacent\'94 were harsh and arbitrary, which is how Bondi Beach came to be protected from the inevitable development of the Layer while Bondi Junction was acid-rained back to the 19
\fs10 \super th
\fs14 \nosupersub  
\fs22 century. And so, a public infrastructure project that dwarfed even Snowy Hydro-Nuclear 6 was embarked upon to generate a series of connected semi-permeable electrostatic bubbles that would protect old-money assets from the same metropolitan climatic hell that old money itself had precipitated. All funded, of course, by the Pay As You Starve taxpayer.
\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 When completed, entry into what became known as The Eastern Suburbs Bubbles became privately policed, which meant it was as efficient as it was \'93commercially influenceable\'94. Arrive without the right genetic markers, credit rating, couture, six generations of paperwork or a valid 
\f3\b\fs18 I WORK HERE
\f1\b0\fs22  card on your tablet, and the Bubble Police would burst your entry aspirations. It was not a place you got into by accident.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The Botanic Gardens and The Domain were enclosed under the western end of The Eastern Suburbs Bubbles and therefore no longer freely accessible to the general public. This was generally well received by non-taxpayers, who campaigned to protect the grounds against vandals, sex predators and agitators in the Ulyssian Greens Party (which was later banned by law as a hate group).\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 In the Domain\'92s early history, it was common ground, a place where anyone might breathe the same air as their betters so long as they didn\'92t breathe it too loudly, or cough up anything unseemly. Later came the speakers\'92 corners, where men hollered about the rights of the worker while, far overhead, the ancestors of the Airborne cruised past dropping investment prospectuses like propaganda leaflets. Its great heritage-listed fig trees, that had been filtering air for centuries, watched convicts spawn hedge fund managers, and murmured share prices for almost as long. In its time, it had held the whole population of the city as they welcomed a passing king, and later howled at a premier they wanted hanged. The Domain was ground you came to when you wanted to worship something, or riot about it. Its lawns had borne those crowds and gone back to being lawns without judging the footwear of anyone who trod on them.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 the High Elvis\'92s ambition to hold a festival in the Domain was ambitious. As requested, Chanta\'92s staff arranged all the palm greasing, bureaucratic paperwork and creative form-filling. Among all the red tape, the one questionnaire the High Elvis completed asked whether any of the guests suffered from altitude deficiency sickness\'97the easiest 
\f3\b NO
\f1\b0  box to tick that the High Elvis had ever encountered.\
The event logistics crew came quietly, in trucks, with an orchestra\'92s worth of production gear on the manifests and something a good deal louder in the crates. 
\f2\i Timpani
\f1\i0 , the paperwork swore. 
\f2\i Choral risers. A modest allocation of woodwind
\f1\i0 . Nobody in a hi-vis vest so much as smirked while forklifting a wall of subwoofers the height of two double-decker buses past a clipboard that called them 
\f2\i acoustic staging for a chamber ensemble
\f1\i0 , or while wheeling in enough amplification to reanimate the dead and indecently proposition them\'97all of it declared as 
\f2\i incidental audio fidelity projectors
\f1\i0 . They worked heads-down, constructing, staging, rigging and roofing a rock cathedral beam by beam, with lighting trees and scaffolding, kilometres of sound cables, and dozens of wireless routers and boosters\'97in the same location where the Airborne held their galas and their executions, often both on the one event card, especially at Christmas and on Good Fridays.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Over the days the stage took shape, not a single official came to check proceedings. The paperwork could have said 
\f2\i classical polka thrash metal
\f1\i0 , because 
\f2\i classical
\f1\i0  quietly proclaimed, 
\f2\i nothing to see here, go back to your homes
\f1\i0 . \
By the night before the festival, a black cliff of scaffold and cloth stood wired for a volume that would shortly put some hard questions to the city\'92s sandstone bedrock about the life it had led. The Domain had, in its centuries, played host to kings and coloraturas, to carols and cricket, and to at least one rock band whose lighting rig drew formal complaints from three separate observatories. It knew it was only rock and roll, but it liked some of it. What it had never once hosted, what no permit in all its heavily audited history had thought to ask about, was whether the audience might be the wrong people\'97the Elvi, or the Cloudborne without a
\fs20  WORK HERE 
\fs22 card between them. Worse: perhaps half of the hundreds of thousands would be Airborne teenagers and young adults, cheerfully indistinguishable from the Cloudborne by the second song. \
The permit said classical. What the city got was an uprising.
